**Convention 5.2 (KI hypotheses and redundant factors).** Koashi and Imoto,
*What is Possible Without Disturbing Partially Known Quantum States?*,
[arXiv:quant-ph/0101144v2](https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/0101144v2),
Theorem 5 (PDF p. 17), treats a possibly infinite state family with
finite-dimensional common support, standard blank ancillary states, an
isometry for faithful transfer, and a prescribed isomorphism from the input
support to a subspace of the output. Its support decomposition is the
maximal state-set structure, not an arbitrarily chosen grouping of states.

In the notation of their equations (85)–(88) (PDF p. 12),

$$
\rho_s=\bigoplus_l p(s,l)\rho_J^{(s,l)}\otimes\rho_K^{(l)},
\qquad
\mathcal H_A=\bigoplus_l\mathcal H_J^{(l)}\otimes\mathcal H_K^{(l)}.
\tag{QC.35}
$$

The redundant-factor state $\rho_K^{(l)}$ is independent of $s$.
Preservation is constrained by both identity action on the $J$ factor and

$$
\operatorname{tr}_E\!\left[
 U_{KE}^{(l)}(\rho_K^{(l)}\otimes\Sigma_E)
 U_{KE}^{(l)\dagger}\right]=\rho_K^{(l)}.
\tag{QC.36}
$$

Dropping (QC.36) would not give their equivalence for state-preserving
operations. Preserving a redundant marginal also does not by itself preserve
an arbitrary pre-existing purification of that marginal.

For the present four-state certificate, the bridge to this structure is
explicit. A pure block-diagonal state in (QC.35) occupies exactly one direct
summand. Nonzero overlaps in (QC.27) force the four states into the same
summand. A pure tensor-product state has pure factors, so the common
$\rho_K$ is rank one. The common support is the span of the actual states,
which by (QC.28) is all of $V$. Hence there are no additional occupied
summands, and no redundant-factor directions beyond that one-dimensional
support. The nonclassical factor has dimension three. The elementary
dilation calculation (QC.29)–(QC.31) states this special case without
silently imposing another decomposition.

The known isomorphism for faithful transfer is the calibrated identification
by $W$ in (QC.5), restricted to the live space. KI applies to the composite
encode–decode transfer with standard ancillas; the intermediate block bound
still needs the finite-branch rank argument above. Neither KI Theorem 5 nor
equations (85)–(88) directly state the connected-overlap dimension conclusion
used for these source states. The arbitrary actual reference is preserved
here by (QC.13) and (QC.17), which hold on the entire supported operator
algebra; it is not inferred from a marginal-only use of KI.

The pure-state no-imprinting, support compression, Stinespring and Choi
arguments in these proofs are `literature-attested` mature ingredients.
Their related joint-support use for a different emission source is already
present in [Phase Boundary](RECURSIVE_RELATIONAL_OBSERVATION_PHASE_BOUNDARY.md),
§2. That source's emission law and capacity are not transferred. The
calculations (QC.10), (QC.17), (QC.24)–(QC.28) and their use in the exact
original-control coding task are `repo-derived` source-specific deductions.
